o build a capitol city for him, where the streets are of polished tin and copper, and the walls are made of basalt and obsidian. He declared that this city must be unique within the boundaries of Earth and Sky, for he would brook no competitors to his glory.<br /> The architects began their design, hastened by the knowledge that any failure would cause them to lose their heads. They layered civil wards over runes of prosperity, shaping the street-maps and sewers into a web of mystical power.<br /> These plans they bound into a thick book, and they carved the founding stone:<br /> \u201cHere is MTUN MTARC, Capitol of the World\u201c.<br /> This stone was placed on the spot where MTUN MTARC was to be built, and the shanty-town of builders and hedge-wizards grew around it. Until, two months later, the king and his architects got word that no one was building MTUN MTARC.<br /> Rushing to the shanty-town, they found it empty. An investigation of the huts showed the truth - their closets and cupboards had become gateways to the great and teeming city MTUN MTARC. The people (much of whom weren\u2019t true) bowed to the king and paid him homage, and he made MTUN MTARC his capitol.<br /> Truly, MTUN MTARC is without equal - MTUN MTARC is described by and describes its name. Any attempt to build a copy of MTUN MTARC merely expands the city itself - any structure, natural or artificial, built within 10 leagues of the founding stone becomes a gateway to that great and terrible city. The approach to the gateways is a place of unnaturally straight rivers and animal trails as wide and linear as any road built by human hands.<br /> Sometimes, men and women dream of MTUN MTARC, and set to building feverishly in their nightclothes, their bare feet cut and bruised as they dig in gravel and dirt. Oftentimes, they die from exhaustion or accident before they finish the first structure - those who succeed soon find their village claimed by MTUN MTARC.<br /> All roads lead to MTUN MTARC - 